<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acanthus (Greece)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acanthus was an ancient Greek city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> peninsula in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, near the isthmus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, founded by colonists from Andros around 655 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>' army passed it in 480 BCE while digging a canal through the isthmus, and in 424 BCE it went over to the Spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasidas">Brasidas</a>. Its appeal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in 382 BCE led to the war that broke up the Chalcidian League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acanthus-greece.jpg" alt="Acanthus (Greece)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christaras A · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ierissos_village,_Stagira-Akanthos_municipality,_Chalkidiki_prefecture,_Greece_-_Ancient_Akanthos_house_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
